# Symbols the docs may name that this tree does not contain.
#
# The symbol checks assert that a backticked identifier exists somewhere in the tree,
# which is what catches a rename the prose did not follow. A few names legitimately
# fail that: a platform API mcfish does not call, named while explaining what a port
# to that platform would need.
#
# KEEP THIS SHORT AND ARGUED, on the same terms as tools/transcript_known.txt: every
# line is a hole in a gate, so it needs a reason that survives being read aloud.
#
# EXPIRING BY CONSTRUCTION: an entry here that turns out to EXIST in the tree fails
# the gate. That is the direction this list rots in -- a symbol gets implemented, the
# exemption stops describing anything, and it silently starts covering the rename it
# was never meant to cover.
#
# One name per line, '#' comments and blanks ignored.

# Windows' high-resolution clock, named in 06-platform.md's account of what a Windows
# port would need for `now_ms`. mcfish is POSIX-only and calls clock_gettime; if this
# ever appears in the tree, the port happened and the exemption must go.
QueryPerformanceCounter
